## Decisions

### When's edit form can now switch an event between Invite only and Anyone with the link. Switching to or from Public is refused by the server's write policy for hosts who aren't admins. Should I add policy rules so hosts can do that, with going Public sending the event to admin approval?

**Background.** On Helios When, an event's sharing lives in three `GROUP` cells. `listed` puts it on the calendar for everyone to find. `visible_to` is who can open it: everyone, or the event itself, meaning only its invitees. `members_visible_to` is who can see who's going. The form offers three settings:

| Setting | listed | visible_to | members_visible_to |
|---|---|---|---|
| Invite only | false | the event itself | blank |
| Anyone with the link | false | everyone | everyone |
| Public | true | everyone | everyone |

When a host who isn't an admin adds a Public event, it starts as `status` pending and waits for a When admin to approve it.

**What's done.** `editEvent` (`web/when/writes.js`) now writes those cells when the chosen setting differs from the event's current one. The picker opens on the event's real setting; before, it always showed Invite only for anything not added in When. I tested this on the sample server, signed in as Jordan Whitfield (not an admin), on an event Jordan added called "Sharing Test":

- Invite only → Anyone with the link: saved, the event now reads Link.
- Anyone with the link → Invite only: saved.
- Invite only → Public (classroom Everyone): the form shows **`you may not change GROUP.status`**.

**Why Public fails.** `internal/db/policy.go` has no `set GROUP.listed` rule at all, so a host can never list or unlist an event. A host also can't set `status` to pending, and can't move a pending event back to open: the existing rule (`policy.go:1033`) allows that only for someone who runs the parent category, meaning an admin. Admins never see the picker in the edit form; for them it's the correct-the-school's-version form. So this only affects hosts.

Here is the same event, "Sharing Test" hosted by Jordan, under each option:

- (*) Add host rules to the policy
  Three rules in `policy.go`'s When section, plus a test in `internal/db`:
  ```
  (set GROUP.listed (and (= @old.kind "event") (runs_list @old) (or (not @new.listed) (= @new.status "pending"))))
  (set GROUP.status (and (= @old.kind "event") (runs_list @old) (= @new.status "pending") @new.listed))
  (set GROUP.status (and (= @old.kind "event") (runs_list @old) (= @old.status "pending") (= @new.status "open") (not @new.listed)))
  ```
  - Invite only → Public: saved as listed and pending. Jordan sees it dashed in amber with Waiting for approval, and admins get Approve and Decline, the same as an event added as Public.
  - Public (approved) → Link: unlisted and stays open, so it leaves everyone's calendar but its link still works.
  - Public (still pending) → Invite only: unlisted and goes back to open, live for its invitees.
  - A host can't list an event without it going to pending, so the approval step holds.
- ( ) Leave Public out of editing
  No policy change. The edit form for a host offers only Invite only and Anyone with the link, and a Public event's form hides the picker.
  - Invite only → Public: not offered. Jordan has to add a new Public event.
  - Public → Link: not offered, so a host can't take an event off the calendar. Only an admin can, by Declining it.

